+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `json5` 1.3.1 (callum-oakley/json5-rs)
+
+Setup notes: `cargo add --dev hegeltest --features serde_json` worked first try (hegeltest
+0.28.2, edition 2024 crate, no MSRV friction). `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`. Checked for
+maintainer AI-consent gates before starting: none found (no CONTRIBUTING.md, no
+HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md / .ai-ack, plain MIT license, no anti-AI clause in the README).
+All tests were added to the existing integration test files (`tests/de.rs` for parser-facing
+properties, `tests/ser.rs` for serializer-facing ones); no new test files.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+In `tests/ser.rs` (serialize → deserialize):
+
+1. **`value_roundtrip_and_fixpoint`** — an arbitrary `serde_json::Value` (hegel's
+   `extras::serde_json::values()` generator) survives `to_string` → `from_str` exactly, and
+   the roundtrip is a fixpoint (re-serializing reproduces the string byte-for-byte).
+   Evidence: `src/lib.rs` docs describe `to_string`/`from_str` as a serializer/deserializer
+   pair over the whole Serde data model. Integral-valued floats are replaced by `0.5` by a
+   commented shield (`replace_integral_floats`) because they hit real bug #1 below.
+2. **`float_roundtrip`** — f64 roundtrips bit-exactly, *including* `NaN`, `-NaN`,
+   `±Infinity` (which JSON5 can represent; `serialize_float!`/`parse_number` handle them
+   explicitly). Finite integral floats excluded via `tc.assume` (bug #1; comment references
+   the pin).
+3. **`integer_roundtrip`** — full-range `i128`/`u128` roundtrip (MIN/MAX/0 boundaries drawn
+   by the unbounded generator; the existing `parse_number` unit test pins these as
+   in-contract).
+4. **`string_roundtrip_at_escape_boundary`** — strings built from characters at the writer's
+   escape set boundary (`src/char.rs escape()`: both quote kinds, `\\`, `\n`, `\r`,
+   U+2028/29) plus JSON5 whitespace, controls, surrogate-adjacent codepoints, and arbitrary
+   codepoints roundtrip exactly.
+5. **`char_roundtrip`** — the separate `serialize_char` path (own delimiter logic)
+   roundtrips for boundary and arbitrary chars.
+
+In `tests/de.rs` (parser):
+
+6. **`json_is_a_subset_differential`** — any `serde_json` output (compact *and* pretty) for
+   an arbitrary `Value` parses under json5 to the same value, with serde_json as the oracle.
+   Evidence: crate docs, "backwardsCompatible": "with JSON".
+7. **`parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_text`** — `from_str` never panics on arbitrary Unicode
+   text, across 8 target types (`Value`, `String`, `f64`, `i64`, `bool`, `Vec<i64>`,
+   `HashMap<String,f64>`, `ByteBuf`).
+8. **`parse_never_panics_on_json5_token_soup`** — same, on concatenations of JSON5 grammar
+   fragments (quote/escape/comment openers, `0x`, `Infinity`, dangling `\uD83D`, …) that
+   uniform text would never produce.
+9. **`nested_input_parses_at_bounded_depth`** — balanced arrays/objects mixed to a drawn
+   depth ≤ 200 parse successfully and preserve the measured nesting depth. The cap protects
+   the test process from bug #3 (comment says so and points at the reproducer).
+10. **`hex_literal_parses_exactly`** — full-range `u128`/`i128` through `0x`/`0X`/`+`/`-`
+    hex literals, `format!("{:x}")` as inverse oracle (covers the special-cased
+    `-0x8000…0000` = `i128::MIN`).
+11. **`decimal_literal_matches_rust_float_parser`** — a grammar-directed generator for JSON5
+    decimal literals (leading `.5`, trailing `5.`, `+` sign, `e`/`E` exponents with
+    leading-zero exponents, never banned leading zeros) must parse to the bit-identical f64
+    that Rust's own `f64::from_str` produces for the same string (Rust's grammar is a
+    superset, so it is an exact oracle). This property *found bug #2* on its first run.
+12. **`leading_zero_is_rejected`** (validation/rejection) — `[+-]?0[0-9]+` is always an
+    error, per `ErrorCode::LeadingZero`.
+13. **`unpaired_surrogate_escape_is_rejected`** (validation/rejection) — lone high or low
+    UTF-16 surrogate escapes in all four follow-up shapes are rejected.
+14. **`comments_and_whitespace_are_insensitive`** — inserting drawn runs of comments
+    (`//`, `/* */`) and exotic whitespace (NBSP, BOM, U+2028/29) at the serializer's
+    structural `\n` positions (strings can't contain raw `\n`, so these are guaranteed token
+    boundaries) never changes the parsed value.
+15. **`generated_json5_document_parses_to_expected_value`** — the crown differential: a
+    JSON5 document generated straight from the grammar — comments/whitespace between all
+    tokens, single- and double-quoted strings, `\xXX`/`\uXXXX`/surrogate-pair escapes, line
+    continuations, unquoted identifier keys (using the crate's public `json5::char`
+    classifiers), trailing commas in arrays and objects, hex and `+`-signed numbers — parses
+    back to exactly the `Value` it encodes.
+
+Plus two deterministic KNOWN-FAILURE pins and one `#[ignore]`d reproducer (below).
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test --no-fail-fast`: **39 passed** (24 de + 15 ser + 5 doctests, including all 15
+hegel properties), **2 failed** (both deliberate KNOWN-FAILURE pins for real bugs), **1
+ignored** (process-aborting stack-overflow reproducer).
+
+Validation performed per Run-and-Reflect:
+- **Liveness**: inverted the assertion in `value_roundtrip_and_fixpoint` and
+  `generated_json5_document_parses_to_expected_value`; both failed with the minimal shrunk
+  counterexample (`v = Null`, document `"null"`), then reverted and `.hegel/` deleted.
+- **Guarded branches fire**: with temporary counters at 10x cases, the unquoted-key branch
+  fired 197×, hex-number branches 75×, and `float_roundtrip` accepted the full 1000 cases
+  (the `tc.assume` for integral floats does not starve it).
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all hegel tests at `test_cases = 1000` (case counter confirmed
+  1000+ executions); no failures beyond the two pins; instrumentation reverted.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All three are oracle-independent (roundtrip/abort failures on values the test still holds),
+reproduced deterministically, and pinned:
+
+1. **Integral-valued floats serialize without a float marker** — pinned by
+   `known_failure_integral_floats_serialize_without_float_marker` (tests/ser.rs, FAILING).
+   `serialize_float!` in `src/ser.rs` writes finite floats with a bare `write!("{v}")`, and
+   Rust's `Display` omits `.0`/exponent for integral values. Consequences:
+   `to_string(&1.0f64) == "1"` re-parses as integer 1 (visible through self-describing
+   targets like `serde_json::Value`); `to_string(&-0.0) == "-0"` re-parses as `+0.0` (sign
+   lost even at typed f64 level); and the pinned strongest form:
+   `to_string(&1e300)` is a 301-digit integer string that **`from_str::<f64>` rejects** with
+   "number too large to fit in target type" — the crate cannot re-parse its own output.
+   serde_json avoids all of this by always emitting a float marker (`1.0`, `1e300`).
+2. **Valid JSON5 integer literals above `u64::MAX` cannot deserialize into f64** — pinned by
+   `known_failure_large_integer_literal_rejected_for_f64` (tests/de.rs, FAILING). Found by
+   property 11 on its first default-count run (counterexample `"20000000000000000000"`).
+   `parse_decimal_number` routes any token without `.`/`e`/`E` exclusively through
+   u128/i128, and `deserialize_number!` then calls `visit_u128`, which serde's f64 impl does
+   not accept — there is no f64 fallback. serde_json parses the same literal as `2e19`.
+   Same root design as bug #1's third manifestation, but reachable with plain valid input.
+3. **Stack overflow (process abort) on deeply nested input** — pinned by the `#[ignore]`d
+   `deep_nesting_stack_overflow_reproducer` (tests/de.rs). The recursive-descent parser has
+   no depth limit: `"[".repeat(n) + "]".repeat(n)` aborts the process (fatal runtime error:
+   stack overflow) at n ≈ 2000 for arrays / ≈ 1500 for objects on a default test-thread
+   stack (probed by sweeping depth directly). Kept ignored because it aborts rather than
+   panics and would take the whole suite down. Untrusted config input can crash a process
+   using this crate.
+
+Also observed, same family as #1/#2, excluded with comments in property 11:
+`from_str::<f64>("-0")` returns `+0.0` (routed through i128, negative-zero sign lost).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. Two properties were narrowed after investigation, both narrowings
+being bug-driven exclusions (commented, referencing the canonical pins) rather than property
+weakenings: integral-float exclusion in properties 1/2/14, and the 18-digit cap on
+*pure-integer* forms in property 11's generator (forms with `.`/exponent still use 25
+digits). The depth cap in property 9 protects the test process from bug #3, not the
+library's contract.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Everything in SKILL.md and the Rust reference worked as documented; zero compile-error
+iterations beyond one `unused_imports` warning (imported `Generator` in a file that ended up
+not using combinator methods — the reference's note on this is accurate, I just applied it
+to the wrong file). Specific confirmations:
+
+- `extras.md`'s `serde_json` integration (`json_gs::values()`) was exactly right for this
+  crate and made the roundtrip/differential properties nearly free. No two-instance trap
+  since the crate under test is json5, not serde_json.
+- The "probe recursion depth directly" guidance found the stack overflow immediately; the
+  "process-aborting bugs → `#[ignore]`d reproducer + capped sibling" pattern applied
+  cleanly. The probe was essential — uniform generation would never reach depth 1500.
+- The "serializer roundtrips: generate at the encode-set boundary" advice shaped property 4
+  (chars drawn from `src/char.rs escape()`'s exact set).
+- The guarded-branch liveness check (counting how often unquoted-key/hex branches fire) was
+  worth doing and confirmed the grammar writer exercises its interesting paths.
+- One small gap: the skill's KNOWN-FAILURE guidance describes pinning via boosted
+  generators + raised `test_cases`, but for bugs with a single canonical scalar input a
+  plain deterministic `#[test]` is simpler and strictly more deterministic; the skill could
+  say that explicitly.
+- `cargo test <name1> <name2>` doesn't accept two test names (cargo quirk, not hegel) —
+  cost one retry during the liveness check.
